/// DIAGNOSTIC FALSE POSITIVE (2026-07-15, seed rcdzc, iter 47). A bare nullary-variant pattern in a match
/// NESTED inside another match arm draws two SPURIOUS warnings — CDZ0306 "unused match binding: `TInt` is
/// never used" and CDZ0213 "this match arm is unreachable" — even though the pattern is a CONSTRUCTOR
/// (matched correctly at runtime), not a binding. `cdz check` still exits 0; the program COMPILES and RUNS
/// CORRECTLY. Only the warnings are wrong.
///
///   match a { TInt => (match b { TInt => 1 | TBool => 2 })   -- inner `TInt`/`TBool` are CTORs
///           | TBool => 3 }
///   classify(TInt, TBool) = 2   -- PROVES the inner `TInt` arm is a ctor: if it were an irrefutable
///                               -- binding it would match TBool and return 1, and TBool would be dead.
///
/// SHARP:
///   • the inner match must be NESTED inside another match's arm — the SAME `match b { TInt => 1 | TBool
///     => 2 }` at TOP LEVEL (a def body) is CLEAN, no warning;
///   • the pattern is a BARE nullary ctor name (`TInt`) — the lint mis-classifies it as an irrefutable
///     BINDING (so CDZ0306 "unused binding" fires) which it then thinks shadows the sibling arm (so
///     CDZ0213 "unreachable" fires on `TBool`).
/// So the resolver's lint pass, inside a nested match, reads a bare nullary ctor pattern as a binder even
/// though lowering/emit correctly treats it as a ctor (the runtime value is right). Same root as the
/// dotted-nullary-arm resolver bug (`reject-dotted-nullary-arm-with-nested-sametype-match`), but the
/// dotted form is a hard error while the bare form is a false WARNING + correct code.
///
/// Cosmetic (no wrong value), but noisy — a compiler pass full of nested nullary-variant matches (a
/// type-checker's `teq`, `src/tycheck.cdz`'s inner `match rt { TInt => … | TBool => … }`) drowns in false
/// warnings. WORKAROUND: compare via a scalar `tag` (`match t { TInt => 0 | TBool => 1 }` at top level,
/// then `tag(a) == tag(b)`) so no nullary match is nested. Fix locus: the resolver's pattern-vs-binder
/// classification in a nested-match arm (make it agree with lowering, which reads a bare nullary name as
/// its ctor when the scrutinee type has such a variant).
///
/// This file `cdz check`s with exit 0 (the warnings are non-fatal); its `@test` PASSES.
type Ty =
  | TInt
  | TBool

/// The nested-nullary-match shape that draws the spurious warnings (on the inner `TInt`/`TBool`).
def classify(a: Ty, b: Ty) = match a with
  | TInt => (match b with
    | TInt => 1
    | TBool => 2)
  | TBool => 3

@test
def nested-nullary-match-is-a-ctor-not-a-binding() =
  // classify(TInt, TBool) = 2 proves the inner TInt arm is a CONSTRUCTOR (a binding would return 1)
  if classify(TInt, TBool) == 2 then
    (if classify(TInt, TInt) == 1 then (if classify(TBool, TInt) == 3 then unit else trap("TBool→3")) else trap("TInt,TInt→1"))
  else
    trap("nested TInt is a ctor → 2, not a catch-all binding")

export { classify }

/// RESOLVED 2026-07-15 (trunk@dd77ccc1b): nested nullary-variant resolution fixed — dotted/bare nullary ctor patterns in a nested match resolve correctly (no CDZ0201/false CDZ0306/CDZ0213). cdz check CLEAN.
